## 6. Persistent Analytics Infrastructure

The existing telemetry implementation stores information in:

`/tmp/fylovanta-telemetry.json`

This is unsuitable as the primary source of production analytics because temporary storage can disappear after restarts or deployments.

Replace the production analytics storage with persistent storage using a suitable free-tier database, such as Supabase, where appropriate.

Do not make the application dependent on an unnecessarily complex analytics infrastructure.

Create a suitable schema to track:

- Event ID.
- Event type.
- Job ID.
- Tool slug.
- Tool category.
- Success/failure status.
- Event timestamp.
- Sanitised device category where appropriate.
- Source category where consent permits.

Do not store:

- Uploaded file names.
- Uploaded file contents.
- Personal documents.
- Raw IP addresses.
- Sensitive user information.

Implement:

- Successful processing counts.
- Failed processing counts.
- Tool-wise usage.
- Category-wise usage.
- Daily and monthly totals.
- Duplicate event protection.
- Server-side validation where applicable.
- Appropriate rate limiting.

Use a unique job ID to prevent the same conversion from being counted multiple times.

Do not rely on an in-memory Set as the only long-term duplicate protection mechanism.

The production statistics endpoint must retrieve real persisted data.

Any public usage counter must display only verified successful processing totals.

Never generate fake usage statistics.

Keep local development functional when external database credentials are absent.

